===Etymology 1===
{{PIE word|en|h₁óynos}}{{etymid|en|num}}
From {{inh|en|enm|oon}}, {{m|enm|on}}, {{m|enm|oan}}, {{m|enm|an}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ān|t=one}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*ain}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*ainaz|t=one}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*h₁óynos|t=single, one}}.

Cognate with {{cog|sco|ae}}, {{m|sco|ane}}, {{m|sco|wan}}, {{m|sco|yin|t=one}}; {{cog|frr|ån|t=one}}; {{cog|stq|aan|t=one}}; {{cog|fy|ien||one}}; {{cog|nl|een}}, {{m|nl|één|t=one}}; {{cog|nds-de|een}}; {{cog|de|ein}}, {{m|de|eins|t=one}}; {{cog|da|en|t=one}}; {{cog|sv|en|t=one}}; {{cog|nn|ein|t=one}}, {{cog|is|einn|t=one}}; {{cog|la|ūnus|t=one}} ({{cog|itc-ola|oinos}}); {{cog|ru|оди́н}}, {{cog|es|uno}}. {{doublet|en|a|an|Uno}}.

The use as an indefinite personal pronoun may have been influenced by unrelated {{der|en|fr|on}},<ref name="Etymonline"/> although the Germanic languages widely use cognates for the same sense (usually in non-subject function, but also in subject function, e.g. {{m+|lb|een}}).

Verb form from {{inh|en|enm|onen}}.

