Around the 14th century, in southwest and western England, the word began to be pronounced with an initial {{IPAchar|/w/}}<ref name="Etymonline">{{R:Etymonline}}</ref><ref name="Century">{{R:Century 1911|atone}}</ref> (compare e.g. {{m|en|woak}}, {{m+|enm|wocke}}, a dialectal form of {{m|en|oak}}),<ref>Christopher Upward, George Davidson, ''The History of English Spelling'' (2011), section "O"</ref> and the spellings {{m|enm|won}} and {{m|enm|wone}} began to be found alongside {{m|enm|on}}, {{m|enm|one}};<ref>''[//quod.lib.umich.edu/m/middle-english-dictionary/dictionary/MED30547 Middle English Dictionary]'': "ō̆n"</ref> the {{IPAchar|/w/}}, though initially nonstandard, had become the norm by the 18th century.<ref name="Etymonline"/> In {{m|en|alone}}, {{m|en|atone}}, and {{m|en|only}},<ref name="Century"/> as well as in the dialectal form {{m|en|'un|un, 'un}}<ref name="Etymonline"/> (and in {{m|en|none}} and {{m|en|no}}),<ref>Oliver Farrer Emerson, ''the History of the English Language'' (1921), page 314</ref> the older pronunciations without {{IPAchar|/w/}} are preserved,<ref name="Etymonline"/><ref name="Century"/> while {{m|en|once}} shows the same {{IPAchar|/w/}}.

